# Pin text/EOL behavior so the index stays canonical across machines.
#
# Without this file, git inherits each developer's local `core.autocrlf`
# default. A clone on a machine whose default differs from the committer's
# appears "massively dirty" on first checkout — every text file flags as
# modified solely because of CRLF<->LF drift. Pinning behavior here removes
# that ambiguity.

# Default: treat every file as text, normalise EOL to LF on commit, check out
# as LF on every platform. Linux is the primary host; this matches the index.
* text=auto eol=lf

# --- Binary asset classes - opt out of text handling entirely ---------------
# Astro/Cloudflare sites carry these under public/; without an explicit binary
# marking, EOL normalisation can corrupt them and diffs become unreadable.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.avif binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary

# Fonts (self-hosted under public/fonts)
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.eot   binary

# Media
*.mp4  binary
*.mov  binary
*.webm binary
*.mp3  binary
*.wav  binary

# --- Platform-specific text files that MUST keep CRLF ----------------------
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# --- Lockfile - explicit text so it doesn't slip through as binary ---------
package-lock.json text
